    <statement><p>Convert the following point from rectangular to cylindrical coordinates:
<me>(-3\sqrt{3},3,-1).</me></p>
<p><m>(r,\theta,z) =</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p><alert> Usage: </alert>To enter a point, for example <m>(x,y,z)</m>, type "(x, y, z)".</p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\left(6,\frac{5\pi }{6},-1\right)</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>(x,y,z)=(-3\sqrt{3},3,-1)</m>. We first find <m>r</m>:
<me>r=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}=\sqrt{(-3\sqrt{3})^2+(3)^2}=6.</me>
Since the point <m>(x,y)=(-3\sqrt{3},3)</m> lies in the second quadrant, <m>\frac{\pi}{2}\leq \theta\leq \pi</m>. Hence
<me>\tan \theta=\frac{y}{x}=\frac{3}{-3\sqrt{3}}=-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3},</me>
so <m>\theta=\frac{5 \pi}{6}</m>.



The cylindrical coordinates are thus
<me>(r,\theta,z)=\left( 6, \frac{5 \pi}{6}, -1 \right).</me>


</solution>
